record fuel prices can make filling up  these days feel like robbery at pump  point so in the days of the 100-pound  tank are consumers getting a fair deal  government has asked regulators to  investigate and they found cause for  concern in parts of the market what we  found is there are two main factors  driving the prices up one is the  increase in global crude oil prices that  accounts for about a third of that  overall increase but the other big  factor and this is something that we're  particularly focused on now is that the  gap the difference between that crude  oil price and the wholesale cost of  petrol and diesel coming out of the  refineries that difference has actually  more than tripled over the last year and  that's something we really think is  essential to get to the bottom of that's  why today we're announcing a formal  market study to take our investigation  further  petrol and diesel prices have risen  around 60 pence a liter in a year the  cma found the increase in crude oil  prices and the falling value of the  pound contributed 27 pence  while the gap between the cost of crude  oil going into refineries and the  wholesale price of petrol and diesel  coming out was up 24 p  but the margin between wholesale and  retail prices was largely unchanged at  around 10 pence  the petroleum industry says it will  cooperate with the cma and that it  sympathizes with motorists some of whom  staged a go slow on motorways this week  in protest at soaring prices  drivers are long suffering from  particularly high prices at the pumps at  the moment and we don't feel those  prices are justified the wholesale price  of fuel that's the price which major  retailers buy the fuel in for has been  dropping weekly for weeks and weeks and  weeks now and yet retail pump prices  haven't changed to reflect that the cma  will also examine price differences  between outlets and has urged government  to make retailers share real-time price  information with motorists but many want  ministers to cut duty first  one thing government can do is cut taxes  and this investigation found that a five  percent vat reduction was passed on to  customers at the pumps but that still  leaves 45 of the price of a liter made  up of v80 and fuel duty and without  further big cuts there's not much chance  that prices will come down here anytime  soon  with up to a year before the cma reports  back it looks like a long expensive  summer on the road paul kelso sky news
 

